StoneFossil in-process serving patch (Fossil 2.26)
==================================================

Fossil is designed as one process per HTTP request: it serves a request and
then exit()s, discarding all process state. Stone embeds the Fossil core in a
single long-lived process (the in-process loopback server in
ios/FossilBridge/StoneFossil.c) and calls fossil_main() once per request. That
re-entry is mostly safe because fossil_main() begins with memset(&g,0,...), but
several modules keep *request-scoped* state in file-scope statics that Fossil
otherwise relies on the process exit to clear. Reused across requests, that
stale state causes redirect loops, dangling-pointer crashes and assert()
failures.

This patch adds a small `*_reset_state()` to each affected module and calls them
all from the single HTTP request entry point, cgi_handle_http_request():

  src/cgi.c      cgi_reset_state()    reply status/headers/content, query params
  src/etag.c     etag_reset_state()   the once-per-request ETag buffer
  src/cookies.c  cookie_reset_state() display-prefs cookie cache (dangling ptrs)
  src/style.c    style_reset_state()  submenu buttons/controls, header flags, JS

Scope is deliberately minimal: only statics that hold request-scoped data are
reset. Process-scoped one-time initialization (SQL function registration, table
loads, etc.) is intentionally left untouched. Regenerate the patch if the pinned
Fossil version changes; gen-fossil-sources.sh applies it and fails loudly if it
no longer applies cleanly.

--- a/src/cgi.c
+++ b/src/cgi.c
@@ -801,6 +801,34 @@
   char cTag;                /* Tag on query parameters */
   char isFetched;           /* 1 if the var is requested via P/PD() */
 } *aParamQP;             /* An array of all parameters and cookies */
+
+/*
+** STONE: Reset all request-scoped CGI static state to its initial values.
+**
+** Fossil normally serves one HTTP request per process, so this file-scope
+** state is cleaned up implicitly when the process exits. When Fossil is
+** embedded and fossil_main() is called repeatedly in a single long-lived
+** process (e.g. the in-process loopback server on iOS), that state would
+** otherwise bleed from one request into the next -- most visibly, a 302
+** redirect status and its Location header would persist and poison every
+** subsequent reply. Calling this at the start of cgi_handle_http_request()
+** gives each request a clean slate. Added for the Stone embedding; see
+** scripts/fossil-inprocess.patch.
+*/
+void cgi_reset_state(void){
+  blob_reset(&cgiContent[0]);
+  blob_reset(&cgiContent[1]);
+  pContent = &cgiContent[0];
+  blob_reset(&extraHeader);
+  zContentType = "text/html";
+  zReplyStatus = "OK";
+  iReplyStatus = 200;
+  rangeStart = 0;
+  rangeEnd = 0;
+  nUsedQP = 0;
+  sortQP = 0;
+  seqQP = 0;
+}
 
 /*
 ** Add another query parameter or cookie to the parameter set.
@@ -2125,6 +2153,12 @@
   int i;
   const char *zScheme = "http";
   char zLine[2000];     /* A single line of input. */
+  cgi_reset_state();    /* STONE: clear request-scoped statics before parsing
+                        ** the new request, so in-process reuse (repeated
+                        ** fossil_main calls) starts each request clean. */
+  etag_reset_state();   /* STONE: ditto for the ETag subsystem. */
+  cookie_reset_state(); /* STONE: ditto for the display-preferences cookie. */
+  style_reset_state();  /* STONE: ditto for page-rendering (submenu/header). */
   g.fullHttpReply = 1;
   g.zReqType = "HTTP";
   if( cgi_fgets(zLine, sizeof(zLine))==0 ){
--- a/src/etag.c
+++ b/src/etag.c
@@ -72,6 +72,22 @@
 static int iMaxAge = 0;     /* The max-age parameter in the reply */
 static sqlite3_int64 iEtagMtime = 0;  /* Last-Modified time */
 static int etagCancelled = 0;         /* Never send an etag */
+
+/*
+** STONE: Reset request-scoped ETag state to its initial values.
+**
+** etag_check() asserts zETag[0]==0 ("only call this routine once!"), which
+** relies on the process exiting after each request. In the embedded in-process
+** server (see scripts/fossil-inprocess.patch) fossil_main() runs repeatedly in
+** one process, so this state must be cleared before each request or the second
+** request trips the assertion. Called from the HTTP request entry point.
+*/
+void etag_reset_state(void){
+  zETag[0] = 0;
+  iMaxAge = 0;
+  iEtagMtime = 0;
+  etagCancelled = 0;
+}
 
